========================================================================================
 the stale-secret searches: Stale / least-recently-used secrets, in SPL
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: how many secrets has nothing read in the last eighteen
 months, and which of them is a rotation job still faithfully rewriting?

 STATUS: 1.0 / 1.2 / 1.3 verified against a live Splunk Cloud instance
 (Splunk 10.5). Results matched independently computed ground truth exactly.
 rotation age / the transaction trace / the auth-method traffic search / the dead-mount search are written but unrun. See splunk/README.md for the load steps.

 Assumes the sample loaded as index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit with KV_MODE=json.
 Against your own estate the index and sourcetype will differ; whatever ships your audit
 device into Splunk already names them. Only the first line of each search changes.

 Nested JSON fields are referenced as 'request.path' (single quotes) inside eval/where,
 and as request.path=... in the base search.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.0  Sanity check: did the data land, and does the time range look right?
       RUN THIS FIRST. If earliest is not ~18 months ago, the index froze the old
       events and every age bucket below will be wrong. See README "Retention trap".
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
| stats count AS events,
        min(_time) AS earliest,
        max(_time) AS latest,
        dc(request.path) AS distinct_paths
| eval earliest=strftime(earliest,"%F %T"), latest=strftime(latest,"%F %T")


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.1  Last-read age per secret: the core fold
       This is the query-time version. It rescans the whole window on every run, which
       is exactly the thing you must not do in production. Fine for a demo-sized sample;
       see 1.4 for the summary-index version that is the real answer.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    request.mount_type=kv
    request.operation=read
    request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats max(_time) AS last_read, count AS reads BY secret
| eval age_days = floor((now() - last_read) / 86400)
| eval last_read = strftime(last_read, "%F")
| sort - age_days
| head 100


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.2  THE ANSWER: age buckets, including "never read"
       The inputlookup is what makes "never read" visible. A secret nobody has read
       emits NO audit event, so it cannot appear in the search results; it only exists
       in the baseline inventory. Without this join the most important number is missing.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response
    request.mount_type=kv
    request.operation=read
    request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats max(_time) AS last_read BY secret

| inputlookup append=t vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats max(last_read) AS last_read BY secret

| eval age_days = if(isnull(last_read), -1, floor((now() - last_read) / 86400))
| eval bucket = case(
      age_days <  0,   "7. never read",
      age_days <= 30,  "1. <= 30 days",
      age_days <= 90,  "2. <= 90 days",
      age_days <= 180, "3. <= 180 days",
      age_days <= 365, "4. <= 365 days",
      age_days <= 540, "5. <= 540 days (18 months)",
      true(),          "6. > 540 days")
| stats count AS secrets BY bucket
| sort bucket

/*  HOW TO KNOW THIS IS RIGHT, without a known-good answer to compare against.

    TOTAL must equal the row count of your inventory lookup. If it is lower, the join is
    dropping rows; if it is zero, the lookup did not load at all. Check that before
    reading any bucket.

        | inputlookup vault_secret_inventory.csv | stats count

    "never read" is the number worth being suspicious of, because it is the one number
    that cannot come from audit data. It is inventory MINUS everything seen. So a stale
    or partial inventory understates it, and a missing inventory makes it vanish
    entirely, silently, with every other panel still looking correct.

    Boundary buckets drift by a secret or two as now() advances. That is expected: ages
    are measured from the moment you run the search. Their SUM is stable; the split is
    not. Do not treat a moved boundary as a regression.                                */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.3  The cleanup list: dormant secrets, worst first
       This is the table an auditor or a platform owner actually acts on.
       NOTE: paths only. No secret value is in the audit log to leak (all HMAC'd), which
       is what makes this safe to run against a regulated namespace.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_type=kv request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats max(eval(if('request.operation'=="read", _time, null()))) AS last_read,
        max(eval(if('request.operation'=="update", _time, null()))) AS last_write,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="read"))   AS reads,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="update")) AS writes
        BY secret

| inputlookup append=t vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats max(last_read) AS last_read, max(last_write) AS last_write,
        sum(reads) AS reads, sum(writes) AS writes, values(namespace) AS namespace
        BY secret

| eval read_age  = if(isnull(last_read),  "never", tostring(floor((now()-last_read)/86400))),
       write_age = if(isnull(last_write), "never", tostring(floor((now()-last_write)/86400))),
       reads     = coalesce(reads, 0),
       writes    = coalesce(writes, 0)
| where read_age == "never" OR tonumber(read_age) > 540
| eval verdict = case(
      read_age=="never" AND writes > 0, "ROTATED BUT NEVER READ: pure waste",
      read_age=="never",                "never accessed",
      true(),                           "dormant > 18 months")
| table secret namespace read_age write_age reads writes verdict
| sort - writes

/*  The "ROTATED BUT NEVER READ" rows are the finding that sells this. A rotation job
    faithfully rewriting a credential that no workload has ever consumed is invisible to
    any view that inspects secrets one at a time, because nothing about the secret
    itself looks wrong. It only shows up when you fold the access history.
    This pattern was confirmed on REAL audit data: four TLS-certificate secrets,
    written 300+ times each by a renewal job, read by nothing, ever.
    See docs/how-it-works.md.                                                             */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1.4  PRODUCTION SHAPE: scheduled search into a summary index
       1.1 rescans 18 months of audit data every run. At enterprise volume (tens to
       hundreds of millions of audit events per day) that is not viable. Fold once on a
       schedule, keep the small rolled-up table, query that.

       Schedule: hourly. Then the dashboard searches `index=vault_summary` and returns
       instantly regardless of how much history sits behind it.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_type=kv request.path="*/data/*"
    earliest=-1h@h latest=@h
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats max(eval(if('request.operation'=="read", _time, null()))) AS last_read,
        max(eval(if('request.operation'=="update", _time, null()))) AS last_write,
        count(eval('request.operation'=="read")) AS reads
        BY secret
| collect index=vault_summary sourcetype=vault:secret:lastseen

/*  ...and the dashboard search that reads it back. Note it re-folds the summary rows,
    because each hourly run appends one row per secret touched in that hour.            */
index=vault_summary sourcetype=vault:secret:lastseen
| stats max(last_read) AS last_read, max(last_write) AS last_write, sum(reads) AS reads
        BY secret
| inputlookup append=t vault_secret_inventory.csv
| stats max(last_read) AS last_read, max(last_write) AS last_write, sum(reads) AS reads
        BY secret
| eval age_days = if(isnull(last_read), -1, floor((now() - last_read) / 86400))


========================================================================================
 Adjacent questions the same dataset answers
========================================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 rotation age  Rotation age: secrets not written in N days
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_type=kv request.operation=update request.path="*/data/*"
| eval secret = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path'
| stats max(_time) AS last_write, count AS writes BY secret
| eval rotation_age_days = floor((now() - last_write) / 86400)
| where rotation_age_days > 90
| eval last_write = strftime(last_write, "%F")
| sort - rotation_age_days


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 the transaction trace  Transaction trace: WHO read this secret, WHERE from, WHEN
      Correlates a secret access back to a specific Kubernetes service account or
      CI pipeline: the "who actually consumes this credential" question.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_type=kv request.operation=read
    request.path="*payments*"
| eval secret     = coalesce('request.namespace.path',"") . 'request.path',
       who        = 'auth.display_name',
       k8s_sa     = 'auth.metadata.service_account_name',
       k8s_cluster= 'auth.metadata.kubernetes_cluster',
       pipeline   = 'auth.metadata.pipeline_id'

``` Resolve ONE identity field instead of grouping by several optional ones.
    A Kubernetes login writes service_account_name and no pipeline_id; a CI login
    writes pipeline_id and no Kubernetes metadata. Measured on a 3,755-event
    sample: 3,174 events had k8s only, 581 had pipeline only, and ZERO had both.
    `stats ... BY` DROPS any event with a null grouping field, so grouping by both
    at once returns no rows at all, on any realistic data, silently and forever.
    Never put two mutually exclusive optional fields in the same BY clause. ```
| eval workload = case(
      isnotnull(k8s_sa)   AND k8s_sa   != "", coalesce(k8s_cluster,"no-cluster") . "/" . k8s_sa,
      isnotnull(pipeline) AND pipeline != "", "pipeline:" . pipeline,
      true(), coalesce(who,"unknown"))
| stats count AS reads,
        min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen,
        values(request.remote_address) AS source_ips
        BY secret workload
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,"%F %T"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen,"%F %T")
| sort - reads


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 the auth-method traffic search  Auth method traffic breakdown over time
      Total request counts alone cannot tell you whether a traffic spike is Kubernetes
      workloads or a batch CI pipeline. This breaks it out by auth method.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_class=auth request.operation=update
| where match('request.path', "/login$")
| timechart span=1d count BY request.mount_type


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 the dead-mount search Dead-config hygiene: auth mounts still taking traffic after decommission
      Auth mounts that survived a platform decommission and are still taking logins.
      Leftover config after a migration is the same class of finding as the stale-secret searches: nothing about
      the mount looks wrong until you ask when it was last actually used.
      FRAME RESULTS AS A QUESTION, NOT AN ASSERTION; a quiet mount may still be needed.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit
    type=response request.mount_class=auth
| eval method = 'request.mount_type'
| stats count AS logins, max(_time) AS last_login, dc(auth.display_name) AS distinct_clients
        BY method
| eval last_login = strftime(last_login, "%F %T"),
       days_since = floor((now() - strptime(last_login, "%F %T")) / 86400)
| sort - logins
